== block 1: the certificate (Proposition 2.5) and a human who is not deterministic
  human: answers f(x) with probability 1 - e, one other answer with probability e;
  exact imitator A = P: mismatch rate rho' = 2e(1-e); Pr[no mismatch in m rounds] = (1-rho')^m
  m = ceil(ln 20/(theta delta)) = 300 for theta = delta = 0.1, confidence 95%
    e = 0.0   : rho' = 0.000000;  Pr[certificate is issued] = 1
    e = 0.001 : rho' = 0.001998;  Pr[certificate is issued] = 0.5488
    e = 0.01  : rho' = 0.019800;  Pr[certificate is issued] = 0.00248
    e = 0.1   : rho' = 0.180000;  Pr[certificate is issued] = 1.394e-26
  m = 29958 for theta = delta = 0.01:
    probability that two answers agree = 0.999: Pr[issued] = 9.614e-14
    probability that two answers agree = 0.99: Pr[issued] = 1.734e-131
  check of rho' >= s/2 with s = 1 - sum_y P(y)^2 (used for 'issued with probability <= exp(-m s/2)'):
    100000 random pairs: min of (mismatch - s/2), (mismatch - TV), (TV + s - mismatch) = 1.332e-15
== block 2: Theorem 3.2(a) holds for each fixed Q, not for all Q at once
  X = [N], L = 1, input uniform; event: the input of round T was never asked before
    N =    50, n =  10: probability 0.9146;  L/n = 0.1000
    N =   200, n =  20: probability 0.9539;  L/n = 0.0500
    N =  1000, n =  50: probability 0.9759;  L/n = 0.0200
== block 3: Example 3.3a, rehearsal inputs from a generator with a seed of k = 3 bits
  X = {0..63}; L = 1; human f = 0 (weight 1/2); for each of 8 seeds sigma the function
  g_sigma = 0 on A_sigma, 1 elsewhere (weight 1/16); A_sigma = {x : parity(x & mask_sigma) = 0},
  eight distinct nonzero masks, so |A_sigma| = 32 and |A_sigma & A_s| = 16 for sigma != s.
    n =    10: Pr[deployed answer of M^(T) is wrong] = 0.10050 with the generator;  0.06106 with fresh inputs;  R1 bound K ln 2/n = 0.06931
    n =   100: Pr[deployed answer of M^(T) is wrong] = 0.06006 with the generator;  0.00611 with fresh inputs;  R1 bound K ln 2/n = 0.00693
    n =  1000: Pr[deployed answer of M^(T) is wrong] = 0.05601 with the generator;  0.00061 with fresh inputs;  R1 bound K ln 2/n = 0.00069
  floor with the generator: (1/2)/(2^k + 1) = 0.05556  (g_s is never refuted)
