== block 8: Example 3.2b, the last mixture on a chain of L links
  each round learns the next link with probability 1/L; after L-1 links every run reaches x_L,
  which is asked with probability 1/L per round.  Pr[run of M^(n+1) meets x_L and no question
  lay in {x_L}] = Pr[Bin(n, 1/L) = L-1]; ratio to L/(n+1), maximised over n:
    L =   10: largest ratio 1.32 at n = 98 (L^2 = 100);  0.4 sqrt(L) = 1.26
    L =   40: largest ratio 2.55 at n = 1599 (L^2 = 1600);  0.4 sqrt(L) = 2.53
    L =  160: largest ratio 5.06 at n = 25598 (L^2 = 25600);  0.4 sqrt(L) = 5.06
  for the round T: sum over t of Pr[L-1 links learned and x_L not asked before round t] = L exactly (a geometric wait)
== block 9: Proposition 1.9a (cut and paste; sqrt(H^2) is subadditive over episodes)
  300 random scaffolds with 2 or 3 interleaved episodes and 3 to 5 calls, random samplers C, P:
    largest |H^2(Q_S, Q_T) - H^2(Q_S&T, Q_S|T)| over all pairs of sets = 1.11e-16
    largest sqrt(H^2(Q_C, Q_P)) - sum_i sqrt(H^2(Q_C, Q_C[i<-P])) = 0.000e+00 (must be <= 0 up to rounding;
      it is 0 when only one episode ever speaks); largest ratio of the two sides = 1.000000 (must be <= 1)
    largest H^2(Q_C, Q_P)/sum_i KL_i: 0.3692 with two episodes (bound 1), 0.3621 with three (bound 3/2)
